{
  // Type-checks the TESTS as well as src. The main tsconfig.json is `"include": ["src"]` because it
  // is the BUILD config — it emits to dist/ and must not compile tests into the artifact — so for
  // most of this repo's life NO test file was type-checked at all.
  //
  // That is not cosmetic. `AnonPolicy.categories` is a Record<AnonCategory, boolean>, so adding a
  // category makes every object literal that omits it a type error. That is the tripwire meant to
  // stop a new detector from being silently OFF in the tests that claim to cover it. With tests
  // unchecked the tripwire never fired: CARD, PHONE and NATID were missing from four `categories`
  // literals, so those three detectors were quietly disabled in every screenshot-redaction and
  // anonymization test.
  //
  // Run it: `npm run typecheck`. `npm run build` is unchanged and still emits src only.
  //
  // WHAT THIS CATCHES: every file under src/, tests/ and scripts/. There are NO exclusions — the
  // list of 60 excluded test files and their ~250 accumulated errors was burned down in #385, and
  // `exclude` was deliberately left off rather than emptied so that adding one back is a visible,
  // arguable change to this file instead of one more line on a list. A new test file is checked
  // automatically, because the include is the whole tree.
  //
  // ONE PROJECT, NOT SEVERAL. Everything here runs under `types: ["node"]`: the suite is
  // environment: "node" (vitest.config.ts) and there is no jsdom leg, so there is nothing for a
  // second project to express. The two genuinely browser-side modules the suite imports —
  // public/js/command-palette.js and public/js/graph-view.js — are covered by hand-written .d.ts
  // files next to them that declare only their pure, DOM-free exports; that is what lets a node
  // test import them without pulling lib.dom into everything else. The extension is a separate
  // package with its own tsconfig, and is type-checked by its own CI job.
  //
  // NOTHING IS GENERATED, VENDORED OR FIXTURE DATA. `include` names three source directories, so
  // dist/ and node_modules/ are already out of scope; companion/data/ (ATT&CK, D3FEND, centroids)
  // and companion/examples/ are JSON/YAML and cannot enter a TS program; and the suite has no
  // .ts fixture files — tests/eval's only non-TS file is a README. If a generated .ts ever lands,
  // exclude it HERE with a comment naming the generator, not by loosening a compiler option.
  //
  // WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT CATCH: anything outside the type system. A test asserting the wrong
  // thing, or a category present in a literal but set to `false`, still passes. This closes the
  // "silently missing field" hole, not "the test is wrong".
  //
  // Scope was widened by FILE, never by rule: a blanket loosening of `strict` would have let the
  // AnonPolicy omission through everywhere, which is the failure this exists to stop. The same
  // rule applies to reclaiming anything in future — no `any`, no `@ts-ignore`, no per-file
  // strictness dial.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // No emit, and no rootDir constraint — `tests` lives outside the build's rootDir and this
    // config exists purely to check types.
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": "."
  },
  "include": ["src", "tests", "scripts"]
}
